# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEFECT-01 (2026-07-22, severity CRITICAL): without this file, `git add -A` in
# the parent SGEN repo would commit 4.6 GB of Chrome profile including
# `Login Data`, `Local Storage` and live Reddit session cookies for two accounts.
# Verified with: git check-ignore -v data/chrome-profile/Default/Cookies
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Browser profiles — contain live session cookies. NEVER commit.
data/chrome-profile*/
data/**/Cookies
data/**/Login Data
data/**/Local Storage/
data/**/IndexedDB/

# Per-operator Claude credentials
data/operators/

# Collected content, drafts, and the activity log — local working state.
# The recursive globs matter: data/*.json does NOT match data/approvals/*.json (the single-use
# publish-approval tokens) or any other nested working file (evaluation L6).
data/*.json
data/*.jsonl
data/*.png
data/**/*.json
data/**/*.jsonl
data/**/*.png
# Console-written approval tokens and decision records — local, single-use, never committed.
data/approvals/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERYTHING under data/, including files with no extension.
#
# The rules above match by EXTENSION and by directory, which left extensionless local state
# tracked-able. Found 2026-07-30 when `data/machine-id` — the file that decides which computer
# this clone believes it is (src/machine.ts, db/migrations/0013) — turned up as an untracked
# file about to be committed, alongside `data/accounts/**/*.claim`. Committing the machine id
# would give a second clone this machine's identity, which is precisely what its own comment
# says must never happen ("two clones on two machines must not agree about which machine they
# are"). Nothing under data/ has ever been tracked, so this asserts the intent the comments
# already claimed rather than changing what is stored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/

# Database credentials. db/.env holds POSTGRES_PASSWORD; db/.env.example is the
# committed template and carries no secret.
db/.env

# Every other place a secret actually lands.
#
# `db/.env` above covered the one file that existed when it was written. It did NOT cover a
# `.env` at the repository root — which is the single most conventional place to put one — nor a
# token pasted into a text file while setting up dashboard sync. This repository is PUBLIC, so
# either would be published the moment somebody ran `git add -A`.
#
# The negations matter: three .env-shaped files are committed ON PURPOSE — `db/.env.example` and
# the two `.env.test` files, which name a database and carry no credential. A blanket `.env*`
# would silently drop them from a fresh clone and break `npm test`.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!*.env.example
!.env.test
!*.env.test

# Tokens and keys saved beside the work. Named by SHAPE rather than by exact filename, because
# the file you are about to create is never the one you thought to list.
*TOKEN*.txt
*SECRET*.txt
*token*.txt
*secret*.txt
*.pem
*.key
!electron/build/*.key

# Build output
dist/
node_modules/

# Scratch
*.tmp
*.log
qa/evidence/*.png

# Console UI captures. Written by tools/product/ui.test.mjs when REDBOT_UI_SHOTS is unset;
# they are evidence for one run, not something to keep in step with the code.
tools/product/.ui-shots/

# One-off investigation write-ups. Each answers a question asked on a particular day against
# the tree as it stood that day — cloud-hosting feasibility, the collect→review funnel, deploy
# readiness. They cite file:line and measured figures, so they go stale the moment the code
# moves and there is nothing that keeps them honest. Named individually rather than by a glob:
# a pattern like *-FINDINGS.md would silently swallow the next report somebody DOES want kept.
DEPLOY-READINESS-2026-07-30.md
PIPELINE-FINDINGS.md
WHY-NOT-DEPLOYABLE.md

# Console telemetry, written at runtime. Lives under the data root now (S1); this rule
# keeps the old location from being re-committed if an older build writes there.
tools/operator/run-history.jsonl

# Electron build output and captures — generated, large, and machine-specific.
#
# ANCHORED. Without the leading slash this pattern matches a directory called `release` at ANY
# depth, and on 2026-08-26 it silently swallowed `tools/release/` — a `git add` of a new tool
# refused it as ignored, and an `add -f` would have been the wrong answer. Only the build output
# at the repository root is generated.
/release/
electron/.shots/
.pkgboot*/
.provtest/
.exporttest/
.eboot*/
.pkgtest/
